            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>&lt;b&gt;Observed Air Leakage:&lt;/b&gt;

Overall, the air leakage in your house was quite low, however there are a few locations which could be improved. Since there is also an HRV in the house, you shouldnÂt have any worry about making the house too airtight.

The main culprit for air leakage is the garage door. These doors are always leaky but if it isnÂt being used then you could consider sealing it up with plastic or some other method. 



There were a handful of windows that had some leakage behind the frames. The locations were noted during the site visit. This should be a fairly easy fix by removing the window trim in those areas and spray foaming any gaps. Make sure to use low expanding foam around windows and doors so that the frame doesnÂt bow.

A few of the pot lights had leakage around them, while others didnÂt; this suggests that certain lights werenÂt properly sealed with a vapour barrier boot. Since the air leakage is already quite reasonable this might be low on the priority list since it would entail getting into the attic and 

There was also minimal leakage around outlets and switches on the exterior walls. You can get foam gaskets that you place under the outlet plate that can help reduce leakage. You can also spray foam around the outside of the metal electrical box if there is enough of a gap.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

For a minimum 10% improvement you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 55&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;2.4 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 500 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must have a home energy evaluation, which includes an air leakage test, before and after your retrofits. For a full rebate, the rebate application, with a copy of the first page of both home energy evaluation reports, must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of the second energy evaluation. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</airSeal>
                <ceilings>Ceilings (Gable)



The attic of your house was assumed to have 14 inches (R50) of blown cellulose insulation. I have modelled adding more cellulose insulation to bring it up to R60.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding about 4 inches (R10) blown cellulose insulation to the attic, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 45&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;2.1 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. This wouldnÂt qualify for a rebate but it could coincide with repairing the pot light vapour barrier boxes if you ever decide to do that.



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</ceilings>
                <HeatingSystem>PELLET BOILER



The small district heating system you currently have between the shop and the house would make it easy to incorporate a pellet or cord wood boiler. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding a pellet boiler onto your heating system with an 85% AFUE, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 560&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 5000 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Alternative Energy Technologies Program. 

For a cord wood boiler the savings could potentially be much greater, but the maintenance is also quite a bit more. It requires multiple loads of wood each day and generates more ash. The savings shown is for a pellet boiler.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Alternative Energy Technologies Program rebate, you must apply and receive pre-approval. The grant amount differs according to the purchase cost of the system, 1/3 of purchase cost, up to a maximum of $2,500 per year. Applicants are encouraged to contact the AEA for pre-approval prior to installation. Projects will be funded on a first come, first serve basis until the program is fully subscribed. Projects must be installed and operational to receive the rebate. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/alternative-energy-technologies-program



-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables>SOLAR UPGRADE



Harnessing solar energy in the NWT may seem unlikely. The short and dark days of winter provide little or no usable energy, depending on the latitude in the NWT. However, the long hours of sunlight in spring and summer can provide usable heat and electricity for up to eight months of the year.  As prices continue to drop, solar PV panels are becoming competitive with the cost of diesel generation. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By installing a 3 kW solar photovoltaic system, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 750&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;9.4 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 2,500 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Alternative Energy Technologies Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Alternative Energy Technologies Program rebate, you must apply and receive pre-approval. The grant amount differs according to the purchase cost of the system, 1/3 of purchase cost, up to a maximum of $2,500 per year. Applicants are encouraged to contact the AEA for pre-approval prior to installation. Projects will be funded on a first come, first serve basis until the program is fully subscribed. Projects must be installed and operational to receive the rebate. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/alternative-energy-technologies-program&lt;/b&gt;



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</Renewables>
                <AdditionalComments>ADDITIONAL COMMENTS



&lt;b&gt;Fuel Costs Â Hay River&lt;/b&gt;

The following values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $18.60 min, $0.30 per kWh

Oil  - $0.89 per litre 

Propane - $0.50 per litre

Wood - $250 per cord 

Pellets - $5.00 per bag



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Disclaimer&lt;/b&gt;

The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Your Energy Advisor&lt;/b&gt;



Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



&lt;b&gt;Nick Walker

Energy Management Specialist

Arctic Energy Alliance&lt;/b&gt;

Phone: (867) 920-3333

Fax: (867) 873-0303

E-mail: nick.walker@aea.nt.ca



&lt;b&gt;Arctic Energy Alliance: Taking action on high heating and power bills, high transportation costs, and climate change!&lt;/b&gt;

</AdditionalComments>
            </RURComments>
